UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 15, Lesson 2: Unit review and showcase', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"A healthy food showcase brings together the five food groups, balanced plates, everyday and sometimes foods, water and food safety. Choose a clear way to present, such as a poster, a recipe card or a short talk, and use real examples with reasons. Be respectful of different foods, cultures, budgets and allergies when talking about meals.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same idea to a new game or situation, then compare two choices for safety and inclusion.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nWrite two sentences for a showcase saying why a mixed lunch is healthier than a lunch of chips and soft drink. Name food groups in your reasons.\n\nFEEDBACK\nSample: ''A mixed lunch with a sandwich, salad, fruit and water uses four food groups, which give different nutrients. Chips and soft drink mostly give salt, fat and sugar and no variety.'' The reasons name groups and nutrients.\n\nWATCH FOR\nBelieving a healthy diet means giving up the foods you love. A healthy diet is about balance and variety, with enjoyment included.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nMake a showcase about healthy eating for a younger child. Include the five groups, a balanced plate, one sometimes food rule, why water matters and two food safety steps. Present it to a family member and collect two stars and a wish.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one choice you changed and why. Any movement is optional, adult-supervised and can be done in a gentler way or described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"PDHPE","learning_intention":"A healthy food showcase brings together the five food groups, balanced plates, everyday and sometimes foods, water and food safety. Choose a clear way to present, such as a poster, a recipe card or a short talk, and use real examples with reasons. Be respectful of different foods, cultures, budgets and allergies when talking about meals.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Practise and apply","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Clear, safe movement space with an adult nearby for any physical activity","Optional: soft ball, cushion or water bottle"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["PH2-IHW-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Make a showcase about healthy eating for a younger child. Include the five groups, a balanced plate, one sometimes food rule, why water matters and two food safety steps. Present it to a family member and collect two sta Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using a showcase on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Believing a healthy diet means giving up the foods you love.. Correction: Sample: ''A mixed lunch with a sandwich, salad, fruit and water uses four food groups, which give different nutrients.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Believing a healthy diet means giving up the foods you love. A healthy diet is about balance and variety, with enjoyment included. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating a showcase on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with a showcase, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about a showcase with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for a showcase. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for a showcase. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson","Sample: ''A mixed lunch with a sandwich, salad, fruit and water uses four food groups, which give different nutrients","Believing a healthy diet means giving up the foods you love"],"question":"Write two sentences for a showcase saying why a mixed lunch is healthier than a lunch of chips and soft drink. Name food groups in your reasons. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for a showcase; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A healthy food showcase brings together the five food groups, balanced plates, everyday and sometimes foods, water and","Sample: ''A mixed lunch with a sandwich, salad, fruit and water uses four food groups, which give different nutrients","Careful use of a balanced meal and explain why it matte with clear evidence","Believing a healthy diet means giving up the foods you love"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with a showcase?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named a balanced meal and explain why it matte, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Believing a healthy diet means giving up the foods you love","Name a balanced meal and explain why it matte only, without linking it to a showcase","Apply a showcase to new material and name the a balanced meal and explain why it matte used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence"],"question":"Which response best applies a showcase with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses safe, healthy or respectful choices appropriately for this lesson on a showcase. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Use a put-down and call it honesty","Believing a healthy diet means giving up the foods you love","Sample: ''A mixed lunch with a sandwich, salad, fruit and water uses four food groups, which give different nutrients","Choose the fastest option even when it ignores safety"],"question":"Which response best uses safe, healthy or respectful choices for a showcase?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name a body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy and say how it helps with a showcase","Choose the fastest option even when it ignores safety","Use a put-down and call it honesty","Believing a healthy diet means giving up the foods you love"],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about a showcase?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea (A healthy food showcase brings together the five food groups, balanced) with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Use a put-down and call it honesty","Example: Leo''s recipe card ''Rainbow wraps''","Believing a healthy diet means giving up the foods you love","Choose the fastest option even when it ignores safety"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about a showcase?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster","Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue","Check safety and fairness, include others, and explain the healthier choice"],"question":"Which action keeps safe, healthy or respectful choices accurate for a showcase?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name a balanced meal and explain why it matte only, with no linked example","Make a showcase about healthy eating for a younger child","Retype the worked example with no new example","Believing a healthy diet means giving up the foods you love"],"question":"Which independent response best applies a showcase to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about a showcase with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating a showcase on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using a showcase on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for a showcase. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with a showcase, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Believing a healthy diet means giving up the foods you love.. Correction: Sample: ''A mixed lunch with a sandwich, salad, fruit and water uses four food groups, which give different nutrients.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested. Share only what you feel comfortable sharing.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-pdhpe-y3-w15-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA PDHPE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (PH2-*); content-point mapping remains pending human review. Physical activities are optional, adult-supervised and adaptable; no child is required to share personal details.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise a showcase.","Read the mission before you start moving or deciding.","Keep your own safety and everyone''s inclusion in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not keep everyone safe and included."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the cue or rule that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore safety, consent or inclusion.","Prefer a reasoned, respectful choice over one that merely looks fun."],"submit":["Confirm that your reasons and reflection are both present.","Proofread names, steps and safety words.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact cue, rule or choice before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key cues.","Say what each cue does for safety, health or friendship.","A final label alone is weaker than a stated reason."],"teach2":["Compare an unsafe or unkind choice with a better one.","Change the choice or strategy, not only the words.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, game or situation that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original reasons and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill in a new situation."]},"subject":"Healthy, Safe and Active Lifestyles","success_criteria":["I can use a showcase with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify a balanced meal and explain why it matters.","I can practise reasons independently on a new prompt.","I can notice respect for differences needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"A healthy food showcase brings together the five food groups, balanced plates, everyday and sometimes foods, water and food safety.","options":["A healthy food showcase brings together the five food groups, balanced plates, everyday and sometimes foods, water and","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no reason"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about a showcase with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Leo''s recipe card ''Rainbow wraps''. Food groups: grain (wrap), protein (chicken), vegetables (carrot, lettuce, capsicum), milk (cheese). Safety: wash hands and use separate boards. Drink: water. Sometimes food: a small cookie on Fridays. He gives a reason for each choice.\n\nTRY FIRST\nWrite two sentences for a showcase saying why a mixed lunch is healthier than a lunch of chips and soft drink. Name food groups in your reasons.\n\nFEEDBACK\nSample: ''A mixed lunch with a sandwich, salad, fruit and water uses four food groups, which give different nutrients. Chips and soft drink mostly give salt, fat and sugar and no variety.'' The reasons name groups and nutrients.","explain":"A healthy food showcase brings together the five food groups, balanced plates, everyday and sometimes foods, water and food safety. Choose a clear way to present, such as a poster, a recipe card or a short talk, and use real examples with reasons. Be respectful of different foods, cultures, budgets and allergies when talking about meals.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Believing a healthy diet means giving up the foods you love. A healthy diet is about balance and variety, with enjoyment included. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"A balanced wrap.","caption":"Four groups in one meal.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Wrap</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">grain, protein, vegetables, milk</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Drink</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">water</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example: Leo''s recipe card ''Rainbow wraps''. Food groups: grain (wrap), protein (chicken), vegetables (carrot, lettuce, c Then write your own reasons.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.\nJuno: Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example: Leo''s recipe card ''Rainbow wraps''. Food groups: grain (wrap), protein (chicken), vegetables (carrot, lettuce, c Then write your own reasons."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.","options":["Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no reason"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about a showcase with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Leo''s recipe card ''Rainbow wraps''. Food groups: grain (wrap), protein (chicken), vegetables (carrot, lettuce, capsicum), milk (cheese). Safety: wash hands and use separate boards. Drink: water. Sometimes food: a small cookie on Fridays. He gives a reason for each choice. Look at each cue, rule or choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key cues, rules or choices from the example and say what each one does for safety, health or relationships.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful reasoning names the cue, rule or strategy and the reason it helps, not only a label such as \"good\".","explain":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without a reason does not show PDHPE thinking. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Show the reasoning","visual":[{"alt":"Respect for food differences.","caption":"Respect every table.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Respect</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">different foods and budgets</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Allergy</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">plan for safe foods</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison.","options":["A guess with no reason","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect","Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about a showcase with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nBelieving a healthy diet means giving up the foods you love. A healthy diet is about balance and variety, with enjoyment included. Here is a more helpful example: Example: Leo''s recipe card ''Rainbow wraps''. Food groups: grain (wrap), protein (chicken), vegetables (carrot, lettuce, capsicum), milk (cheese). Safety: wash hands and use separate boards. Drink: water. Sometimes food: a small cookie on Fridays. He gives a reason for each choice.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice does not work, then write a safer, kinder or healthier choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nSample: ''A mixed lunch with a sandwich, salad, fruit and water uses four food groups, which give different nutrients. Chips and soft drink mostly give salt, fat and sugar and no variety.'' The reasons name groups and nutrients.","explain":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without changing the choice does not fix the error. Believing a healthy diet means giving up the foods you love. A healthy diet is about balance and variety, with enjoyment included. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example.","options":["Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no reason"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about a showcase with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA healthy food showcase brings together the five food groups, balanced plates, everyday and sometimes foods, water and food safety. Choose a clear way to present, such as a poster, a recipe card or a short talk, and use real examples with reasons. Be respectful of different foods, cultures, budgets and allergies when talking about meals. Now choose a different situation to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe or practise one fresh situation that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new situation should use the same PDHPE idea as the example, but happen in a different place or with different people.","explain":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new situation. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Transfer to a new situation"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 15, Lesson 2: Unit review and showcase","unit_number":3,"unit_title":"Healthy food and nutrition","week":15,"week_in_unit":5,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: Leo''s recipe card ''Rainbow wraps''. Food groups: grain (wrap), protein (chicken), vegetables (carrot, lettuce, capsicum), milk (cheese). Safety: wash hands and use separate boards. Drink: water. Sometimes food: a small cookie on Fridays. He gives a reason for each choice.\n\nWrite what makes this example safe, healthy or respectful, naming the cues, rules or choices used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"A balanced wrap.","caption":"Four groups in one meal.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Wrap</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">grain, protein, vegetables, milk</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Drink</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">water</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Respect for food differences.","caption":"Respect every table.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" 